[38;5;231m⏺[39m Fourth fork: [1mhow other code gets at the store.[0m "Works across the app" needs a wiring decision, because the page
  currently loads one plain script with [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m and has no module system in the browser at all.

  - [1mSecond plain script + a global[0m — add [38;5;153m<script src="src/current-user.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and expose one documented
    object (e.g. [38;5;153mwindow.CurrentUser[39m). Matches the existing style exactly, no build step, and the page keeps working when
    opened directly from disk via [38;5;153mfile://[39m. The boundary is a convention rather than something the language enforces.
  - [1mNative ES modules[0m — switch to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m and use real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. The dependency becomes explicit and
    enforced, which matters as more forms consume the store. Cost: browsers block modules over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so the page
    would have to be served ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar) — double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — rejected; this repo has zero dependencies and no build step, and nothing here needs one.

  I lean toward the plain script and a global. Normally I'd argue for real modules, but the deciding factor is that this
  page has exactly one consumer today and the module switch buys enforcement at the cost of breaking the simplest way
  to run the app. The global is also not a dead end: moving one file from a global to an [38;5;153mexport[39m later is mechanical, and
  the future forms you mentioned are the natural moment to pay for it.
